Steps
1
Build a 200-row synthetic support-ticket dataset with 8 company-specific categories (e.g., 'billing.refund_dispute', 'auth.sso_misconfig') and a 40-row held-out eval split.
from datasets import Dataset
import pandas as pd, random, json
random.seed(0)
CATS = ['billing.refund_dispute','billing.invoice_error','auth.sso_misconfig','auth.mfa_lockout','api.rate_limit','api.webhook_failure','data.export_missing','data.pii_deletion']
templates = {c: [f'Customer says: issue with {c.replace("."," ")}. Details: ticket #{i}' for i in range(30)] for c in CATS}
rows = [{'text': t, 'label': c} for c, ts in templates.items() for t in ts]
random.shuffle(rows)
train, eval_ = rows[:200], rows[200:240]
pd.DataFrame(eval_).to_json('eval.jsonl', orient='records', lines=True)
pd.DataFrame(train).to_json('train.jsonl', orient='records', lines=True)
eval.jsonl has 40 lines; train.jsonl has 200 lines; 8 unique labels in each.
2
Lever 1 — Prompting: classify eval set with Llama-3.1-8B on Groq using a zero-shot prompt that lists the taxonomy inline.
from groq import Groq
import os, json, time
client = Groq(api_key=os.environ['GROQ_API_KEY'])
TAXONOMY = '\n'.join(f'- {c}' for c in CATS)
def classify_prompt(text):
    r = client.chat.completions.create(model='llama-3.1-8b-instant',
        messages=[{'role':'user','content':f'Classify into exactly one label:\n{TAXONOMY}\n\nTicket: {text}\nLabel:'}],
        temperature=0, max_tokens=20)
    return r.choices[0].message.content.strip()
evals = [json.loads(l) for l in open('eval.jsonl')]
t0=time.time(); preds_p=[classify_prompt(e['text']) for e in evals]; lat_p=(time.time()-t0)/len(evals)
acc_p = sum(p.strip()==e['label'] for p,e in zip(preds_p,evals))/len(evals)
print(f'PROMPT acc={acc_p:.2%} lat={lat_p*1000:.0f}ms')
Prints accuracy (typically 50-75%) and per-request latency; notice it struggles with lookalike labels.
3
Lever 2 — Retrieval: index the 200 training examples in Chroma, retrieve top-5 neighbors as few-shot examples, then classify.
import chromadb
from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer
enc = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')
cli = chromadb.Client(); col = cli.create_collection('tix')
train = [json.loads(l) for l in open('train.jsonl')]
col.add(ids=[str(i) for i in range(len(train))], embeddings=enc.encode([t['text'] for t in train]).tolist(),
        metadatas=[{'label':t['label']} for t in train], documents=[t['text'] for t in train])
def classify_rag(text):
    hits = col.query(query_embeddings=enc.encode([text]).tolist(), n_results=5)
    shots='\n'.join(f"Ticket: {d}\nLabel: {m['label']}" for d,m in zip(hits['documents'][0],hits['metadatas'][0]))
    r = client.chat.completions.create(model='llama-3.1-8b-instant',
        messages=[{'role':'user','content':f'{TAXONOMY}\n\nExamples:\n{shots}\n\nTicket: {text}\nLabel:'}], temperature=0, max_tokens=20)
    return r.choices[0].message.content.strip()
t0=time.time(); preds_r=[classify_rag(e['text']) for e in evals]; lat_r=(time.time()-t0)/len(evals)
acc_r = sum(p==e['label'] for p,e in zip(preds_r,evals))/len(evals)
print(f'RAG acc={acc_r:.2%} lat={lat_r*1000:.0f}ms')
RAG accuracy jumps notably (often 85-95%) with modest latency increase — this is your 'do you even need fine-tuning?' moment.
4
Lever 3 — Fine-tune: LoRA-tune Llama-3.2-3B on the 200 training rows with Unsloth (rank 16, 2 epochs).
from unsloth import FastLanguageModel
from trl import SFTTrainer
from transformers import TrainingArguments
model, tok = FastLanguageModel.from_pretrained('unsloth/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct', max_seq_length=512, load_in_4bit=True)
model = FastLanguageModel.get_peft_model(model, r=16, lora_alpha=16, target_modules=['q_proj','k_proj','v_proj','o_proj'])
def fmt(ex): return {'text': f"<|user|>Classify: {ex['text']}<|assistant|>{ex['label']}<|end|>"}
ds = Dataset.from_list(train).map(fmt)
trainer = SFTTrainer(model=model, tokenizer=tok, train_dataset=ds, dataset_text_field='text',
    args=TrainingArguments(per_device_train_batch_size=4, gradient_accumulation_steps=2,
        num_train_epochs=2, learning_rate=2e-4, output_dir='out', logging_steps=10, fp16=True))
trainer.train()
Training loss drops below 0.3; adapter saved to ./out. GPU memory stays under 15GB on T4.
5
Evaluate the fine-tuned model on the same 40-row eval set, capturing accuracy and latency.
FastLanguageModel.for_inference(model)
def classify_ft(text):
    ids = tok(f'<|user|>Classify: {text}<|assistant|>', return_tensors='pt').to('cuda')
    out = model.generate(**ids, max_new_tokens=20, temperature=0.0, do_sample=False)
    return tok.decode(out[0][ids.input_ids.shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True).split('<')[0].strip()
t0=time.time(); preds_f=[classify_ft(e['text']) for e in evals]; lat_f=(time.time()-t0)/len(evals)
acc_f = sum(p==e['label'] for p,e in zip(preds_f,evals))/len(evals)
print(f'FT acc={acc_f:.2%} lat={lat_f*1000:.0f}ms')
Fine-tuned accuracy printed; compare to RAG — if FT beats RAG by <3pp, the delta likely doesn't justify the ops cost.
6
Build the decision matrix and write a go/no-go memo weighing accuracy delta vs ops burden (weights hosting, eval drift, base-model upgrades, lock-in).
import pandas as pd
# Rough cost model: Groq ~free; FT self-hosted ~$0.10/hr T4 = $0.000028/sec
cost_groq = 0.00  # free tier
cost_ft_per_call = (lat_f) * 0.000028
df = pd.DataFrame([
  {'lever':'Prompt',    'acc':acc_p, 'lat_ms':lat_p*1000, '$/1k':cost_groq, 'ops':'none', 'lock_in':'none'},
  {'lever':'RAG',       'acc':acc_r, 'lat_ms':lat_r*1000, '$/1k':cost_groq, 'ops':'index refresh', 'lock_in':'low'},
  {'lever':'Fine-tune', 'acc':acc_f, 'lat_ms':lat_f*1000, '$/1k':cost_ft_per_call*1000, 'ops':'weights+evals+drift+GPU', 'lock_in':'high'}])
print(df.to_markdown(index=False))
df.to_csv('decision_matrix.csv', index=False)
memo = f'''# Go/No-Go Memo\nBest accuracy: {df.loc[df.acc.idxmax(),"lever"]} @ {df.acc.max():.1%}\nAccuracy delta FT vs RAG: {(acc_f-acc_r)*100:+.1f}pp\nRecommendation: {"Ship RAG" if acc_f-acc_r < 0.03 else "Fine-tune justified"} — FT adds weights hosting, eval pipeline, drift monitoring, and blocks easy base-model upgrades.'''
open('MEMO.md','w').write(memo); print(memo)
decision_matrix.csv and MEMO.md exist; memo explicitly names the delta and the ops tradeoffs.
